  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{\u2032} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t\\,j))^{\u2032} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{\u2032} \\\\  & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t\\,j)^{\u2032} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-12\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t)^{2}+(-12\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}\u20612t+144{sin}^{2}\u20612t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,\u21d2\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={2}^{2}\\cdot 3=12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$",
